2 CONT García II Sánchez of Gascony
2 CONT
2 CONT García II Sánchez (Basque: Gartzia Antso, French: Garsie-Sanche le Tors or le Courbé, Gascon: Gassia Sans, Latin: Garsia Sancius Corvum, died circa 930), called the Bent, was the duke of Gascony from sometime before 887 to his dea
2 CONC th.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was probably a son of Sancho Sánchez or of Sancho Mitarra, though older sources give a genealogy with a Spanish origin.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His ancestry is, in the end, unknown. He may have been a cousin of Arnold, who some sources claim acted as regent during his minority following his father’s death in 864 (if his father was Sánchez). Other sources place Arnold as S
2 CONC ancho’s successor and date his death to that same year. Whatever the case, García was in power by 887.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that year, he appeared in a charter issued by the grandees of Aquitaine assembled at Bourges to decide on a course of action in the twilight of the reign of Charles the Fat. In 904, he was using the title comes et marchio in li
2 CONC mitibus oceani: "count and margrave to the limits of the ocean." García was the first of a line of dukes which ruled Gascony until 1032 and incorporated the county of Bordeaux into its demesne. García’s daughter Andregoto married 
2 CONC one Raymond, who fathered William the Good, Count of Bordeaux. García divided his domain between his three sons by Amuna:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho IV inherited a rump duchy of Gascony with the ducal title
2 CONT
2 CONT William inherited the county of Fézensac (including Armagnac)
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold inherited the county of Astarac
2 CONT
2 CONT Andregoto, married William the Good, Count of Bordeaux
2 CONT
2 CONT Garsinda, married Raymond III Pons, Count of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Acibella, married Galindo II Aznárez, Count of Aragon
2 CONT
