2 CONT -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaida_of_Seville - '''Zaïda of Seville''' (1070 – 13 Sep 1107) was a refugee Muslim princess who was a mistress later queen of Alfonso VI of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''She might have been identical with Alfonso's wife [https://www.geni.com/people/Isabel-de-France/6000000022332766959 Isabel (Elisabeth)], who bore him two daughters, and died in 1107 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Zaïda is from the Arabic "Sayyida," the female form of Sayyid ("Master"). She is said to have been a descendant of the Prophet Muhammad, but at least one generation in the chain was a 16th century invention. Her tombstone
2 CONC , erected long after her death, says, "Aqui descansa la reina Isabel, mujer del rey Alfonso, hija de Aben-Abeth, rey de Sevilla; que antes se llamaba Zayda," which translates as "here lies Queen Elizabeth, wife of King Alfonso, da
2 CONC ughter of Aben-abeth, king of Seville; previously called Zaïda."
2 CONT
2 CONT Zaïda was a daughter-in-law (and probably also niece) of [http://www.geni.com/people/Abenabed-Muhammad-ibn-Abbad-al-Mutamid-hajib-de-Sevilla/6000000003493151484 al-Mutamid], ruler of the taifa of Seville. Her first husband was her
2 CONC  cousin [http://www.geni.com/people/Abu-Nasir-al-Fatah-al-Mamun-emir-de-C%C3%B3rdoba/6000000012181868591 Fath al-Ma'mum], the ruler of Córdoba and son of the Emir of Seville. He was killed in 1091 while trying to leave Córdoba, wh
2 CONC ich was being beseiged by a group of radical Islamists led by Yusuf ibn Tasufin. Zaïda had been sent to the castle at Almodovar del Rio for safety, and thus escaped. After the death of her first husband, she took refuge with her u
2 CONC ncle in Seville. The Almoravides invested Seville in June and took it in September. At some point in the year, perhaps when Alvar Fanez was in Andalusia vainly trying to relieve the city, Zaïda made her way as a refugee to the cou
2 CONC rt of Alfonso VI. He was already mature (age 51), married to a queen who was ill, and was lacking a male heir. Zaïda became his concubine, converted to Christianity, and took the Christian name Isabel. She bore Alfonso his only su
2 CONC rviving son Sancho. It is not clear whether Alfonso subsequently married her.
2 CONT
2 CONT She might have been identical with Alfonso's wife [http://www.geni.com/people/Isabel-de-France/6000000022332766959 Elisabeth], who bore him two daughters, and died in 1107. She died on 12 or 13 September in childbirth, but the yea
2 CONC r is not known. If she died during the birth of her son Sancho, then she died in 1093. Other dates proposed by scholars are 1099 and 1101.
2 CONT
