2 CONT
2 CONT Odo I (1058 – 23 March 1103), also known as Eudes, surnamed Borel  and called the Red, was Duke of Burgundy between 1079 and 1103. Odo was the second son of Henry of Burgundy and grandson of Robert I. He became the duke following 
2 CONC the abdication of his older brother, Hugh I, who retired to become a Benedictine monk. Odo married Sibylla of Burgundy (1065–1101), daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy.
2 CONT They had:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Florine of Burgundy 1083–1097, wife of Sweyn the Crusader, prince of Denmark
2 CONT     * Helie of Burgundy 1080–1141, wife of Bertrand of Toulouse and William III of Ponthieu
2 CONT     * Hugh II of Burgundy 1084–1143
2 CONT     * Henry 1087–1131, a priest
2 CONT
2 CONT An interesting incident is reported of this robber baron by an eyewitness, Eadmer, biographer of Anselm of Canterbury. While Saint Anselm was progressing through Odo's territory on his way to Rome in 1097, the bandit, expecting gr
2 CONC eat treasure in the archbishop's retinue, prepared to ambush and loot it. Coming upon the prelate's train, the duke asked for the archbishop, whom they had not found. Anselm promptly came forward and took the duke by surprise, say
2 CONC ing "My lord duke, suffer me to embrace thee." The flabbergasted duke immediately allowed the bishop to embrace him and offered himself as Anselm's humble servant.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a participant in the ill-fated Crusade of 1101.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Personnages des Croisades: Premième Croisade - 1096-1099==
2 CONT [http://www.templiers.net/personnages-croisades/index.php?page=personnages_croisade_1#0476]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eudes Ier duc de Bourgogne, « dit Borrel », (1058—1102-03).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ce prince, arrière-petit-fils du roi Robert-le-Pieux, ayant fait le voyage de la Terre-Sainte, mourut à Tarse, en Cilicie, l'an 1102-03.
2 CONT
2 CONT Les armes qu'on lui donne ici, « bandé d'or et d'azur de six pièces, à la bordure de gueules », sont celles qu'ont portées les princes de la première maison de Bourgogne jusqu'à son extinction, en 1361.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe-le-Hardi, quatrième fils du roi Jean, en qui commença la seconde maison de Bourgogne, écartela son écu de France et de Bourgogne; et cet écu se chargea après lui des armoiries des diverses souverainetés acquises par ses t
2 CONC rois successeurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources:''' Versailles, Salles des Croisades — CH. Gavard Editeur rue du Marché Saint-Honoré Paris IV
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Translation:''' "This prince, great-grandson of King Robert-the-Pious, having traveled to the Holy Land, died at Tarsus, in Cilicia, in the year 1102-03.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The arms given to him here, "bandaged or and azure of six pieces, bordered gules", are those worn by the princes of the first house of Burgundy until its extinction, in 1361.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Philippe-le-Hardi, fourth son of King John, in whom began the second house of Burgundy, quartered his shield of France and Burgundy; and this shield assumed after him the coats of arms of the various sovereignties acquired by his
2 CONC  three successors."
