0 @N6000000000437492030@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Aragon
0 @N6000000000945071678@ NOTE Andregoto var arvegrevinne av Aragon. 
1 CONT Galindo Aznaréz var den annen greve av Aragon. Han ga mellom 795 og 815 Jaca
1 CONT den første grunnlov. Det synes å være sikkert at far til Andregoto også het Galindo Aznaréz,
1 CONT den her omtalte Galindo var muligens hennes fars farfar.
0 @N6000000003051221404@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Andregota Galíndez", retrieved 5 Oct 07]
1 CONT Andregota Galíndez was Countess of Aragón from 922 to 925, being daughter of Count Galindo II Aznárez by his second wife, Sancha Garcés of Pamplona. She married García III, king of Pamplona. After 925, Aragón was added to the king
1 CONC dom of Pamplona, until the later split that created the Kingdom of Aragón and the kingdom of Navarre. García, who was her first cousin, divorced Andregota due to consanguinity, and remarried. Andregota apparently remarried and had
1 CONC  further children, although the details have not been discovered. Endregota, wife of 11th century count Sancho Maceratiz, was a descendant of this later marriage.
0 @N6000000000437407465@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Count Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Navarre
0 @N6000000003051221550@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Jimeno I of Pamplona", retrieved 5 Oct 07]
1 CONT Jimeno I (Ximeno)(d. (it is said) 860) is known directly only from a single historical record. In 850, the French court received envoys from Induo and Mitio, "dukes of the Navarrese". These names are thought to represent Íñigo Ari
1 CONC sta and Jimeno, father of García Jiménez. The Códice de Roda lists García Jiménez as ruler of "another part of the kingdom" of Pamplona, and the above record has led to the speculation that Jimeno too held a role of regional co-ki
1 CONC ng or sub-king. The location of this principality has been suggested to have been around Álava, where a count Vela Jiménez, traditionally thought to have been his son, held sway.
1 CONT
1 CONT He has sometimes been described as Jimeno the Strong, but this results from confusion with a much earlier man of that name. Likewise, he sometimes appears as Jimeno Garcés due to hypotheses about his origins. The belief that he wa
1 CONC s kinsman of Íñigo I Arista has led to various reconstructed pedigrees, filling the gaps with otherwise unknown or chronologically misplaced individuals, the most common version making him son of a García Jiménez, and thereby maki
1 CONC ng Garcés Jimeno's patronymic.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was apparently succeeded in his principality by son García Jiménez. In the 10th century, Jimeno's house?the Jiménez?completely displaced the line of Arista and reigned in Navarre until 1234.
1 CONT
1 CONT No record of his wife remains, although historian Justo Pérez de Urbel has suggested he was the unnamed prince of Pamplona to whom princess Leodegundia Ordoñez of León married. The following children have been suggested for Jimeno
1 CONC :
1 CONT
1 CONT García Jiménez, who apparently succeeded him as co-king and was regent during the captivity of Fortún Garcés
1 CONT Íñigo Jiménez, named as brother of García Jiménez in the Códice de Roda.
1 CONT Vela Jiménez, founder of the Vela clan, the connection is not directly attested, but based primarily in his patronymic, geography and chronology.
1 CONT Oneca, wife of Galician count Diego Fernandez, having children Jimeno and Leodegundia, this identification is linked to the hypothesis that Leodegundia Ordoñez was wife of Jimeno I.
0 @N6000000000437123741@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Viero
0 @N6000000000945071425@ NOTE Ana (Aba, Onia, Sancha) var en frankisk prinsesse. 
1 CONT Ana og Garcia hadde foruten Sancho, døtrene Urraca, Velasquita og Elvira. For den
1 CONT eldste datteren, Urraca, stiftet de klosteret Covarrubias. De øvrige døtrene ble begge, etter
1 CONT hverandre, dronninger av Leon som Bermudo II's gemaliner.
0 @N6000000000437474997@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Ribagorza
0 @N6000000000437099381@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Ribagorza
0 @N6000000000437592062@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of Léon
0 @N6000000000945071332@ NOTE Greve av Castilla 995 - 1021. 
1 CONT Sanco Garcéz etterfulgte sin far som greve av Castilla i 995. Han betalte tributt til
1 CONT Almanzor og levde derfor i fred med maurerne inntil han forenet seg med Leons og Navarras
1 CONT folk ved Calatañazor.
1 CONT Da Alamanzor døde i 1008 tok han tilbake Sepulveda og de andre landdeler han
1 CONT hadde måttet avstå. Fra 1012 levde han i et dårlig forhold til kongen i Leon.
1 CONT Han døde antagelig i 1017, muligens i 1021, og ble bisatt i Oñas kloster som var stiftet
1 CONT for ham i 1011. Sagnet forteller at hans mor Oña (Ana) hadde gitt ham en giftig drikk.
0 @N6000000002187755468@ NOTE Sancho I of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho I García (d. 1017), called of the Good Laws (in Spanish, el de los Buenos Fueros), was the count of Castile and Álava from 995 to his death. He was the son of Count García Fernández, against whom he rebelled with the suppor
1 CONC t of Almanzor, the caliph of Córdoba. He was succeeded by his own son García.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT García I Fernández Count of Castile
1 CONT 995–1017 Succeeded by:
1 CONT García II Sánchez
0 @N6000000000945071472@ NOTE Regent av Leon 999 - 1015. 
1 CONT Ved kong Bermudos død gikk tronen til sønnen Alfonso V, men Elvira regjerte i hans
1 CONT navn til 999. Som regent presiderte hun i 1001 i Bodeva ved en forsamling av dommere og
1 CONT adelsmenn. Hun synes å ha vært en dyktig regent og var forøvrig et mønster blandt dronninger
1 CONT og mødre.
1 CONT De spanske kronister beretter at hun slo tilbake et angrep av maurerne. Hun bygde opp
1 CONT igjen Leon som delvis hadde blitt ødelagt av Almanzor.
1 CONT Da hennes sønn ble myndig, trakk hun seg tilbake til et kloster og døde der. Dette må
1 CONT ha funnet sted etter 1015.
0 @N6000000001353965086@ NOTE Sancho II Garcés, also called SANCHO ABARCA (d. c. 994), king of Pamplona (Navarre) from 970, Count of Aragon, and a son of García I (or II). He was defeated by the Moors in 973 and 981 when allied with Castile and Leon. He then s
1 CONC ubmitted to the caliphate, one of his daughters marrying the chief minister of Córdoba, Abu 'Amir al-Mansur, and becoming a Muslim. Sancho visited Córdoba in 992 to pay homage to al-Mansur.
0 @N6000000001354524081@ NOTE Sancho II Garcés, also called SANCHO ABARCA (d. c. 994), king of Pamplona (Navarre) from 970, Count of Aragon, and a son of García I (or II). He was defeated by the Moors in 973 and 981 when allied with Castile and Leon. He then s
1 CONC ubmitted to the caliphate, one of his daughters marrying the chief minister of Córdoba, Abu 'Amir al-Mansur, and becoming a Muslim. Sancho visited Córdoba in 992 to pay homage to al-Mansur.
0 @N6000000001355774081@ NOTE Sancho II Garcés, also called SANCHO ABARCA (d. c. 994), king of Pamplona (Navarre) from 970, Count of Aragon, and a son of García I (or II). He was defeated by the Moors in 973 and 981 when allied with Castile and Leon. He then s
1 CONC ubmitted to the caliphate, one of his daughters marrying the chief minister of Córdoba, Abu 'Amir al-Mansur, and becoming a Muslim. Sancho visited Córdoba in 992 to pay homage to al-Mansur.
0 @N6000000002187750502@ NOTE Sancho II of Pamplona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho II Garcés Abarca (after 935 – December 994) was the Jiménez King of Pamplona and Count of Aragon from 970 until his death. He was the son of García III and Andregota, daughter and heiress of Galindo II Aznárez, Count of Ara
1 CONC gon. After his succession, he recognised his younger brother Ramiro as King of Viguera.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Historia General de Navarra by Jaime del Burgo says (referencing in turn the Anales del Reino de Navarra of José de Moret) that on the occasion of the donation of the villa of Alastue by Sancho to the monastery of San Juan de 
1 CONC la Peña in 987, he titled himself "King of Navarre," the first time that title had been used. In other places, he appears as the first King of Aragon and in others the third. These titles, however, did not come into common usage u
1 CONC ntil the late eleventh century. The epithet "Abarca," meaning "sandal," is not contemporary, but is medieval.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Sancho and his immediate successors, Navarre reached the height of its power and its largest size. During this period, the Navarre was united to the Kingdom of León and the County of Castile by familial bonds. The Navarrese 
1 CONC monarchy supported the young Ramiro II when he secured the throne of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of the Caliph of Cordoba, Al-Hakam II, in 976, and the succession of his son Hisham II, who had been taught by Al-Mansur Ibn Abi Aamir, the prospects of the Christian kingdoms seemed dim. The troops of Al-Mansur def
1 CONC eated the Christians at Torrevicente, south of Soria. Afterwards, the Muslims returned to triumph at Taracueña, near Osma. In 975, Sancho was defeated by the Moors at San Esteban de Gormaz, and in 981 at Rueda, a dozen kilometers 
1 CONC from Tordesillas, the Christians suffered another humiliating defeat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because he could not defeat Al-Mansur by arms, Sancho went to Córdoba as an ambassador for his own kingdom, bringing many gifts for the victorious Al-Mansur, making a pact with him and agreeing to give the Muslim his daughter Urra
1 CONC ca in marriage. From this union was born Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo, the second successor of Al-Mansur who tried to usurp the Caliphate of Córdoba from the Umayyad heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 972, he founded the monastery of San Andrés de Cirueña. In 976, at the monastery of Albelda, the cultural and intellectual centre of his kingdom, the Codex Vigilanus was completed. It is one of the most important illuminated ma
1 CONC nuscripts of medieval Spain, containing the canons of the Councils of Toledo, a copy of the Liber Iudiciorum, and the first Western representation of the Arabic numerals, among many other texts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho married Urraca, the daughter of the Castilian count Fernán González and Sancha, Sancho's aunt. The marriage occurred after 962 and before 970. Before 950, Urraca had been married twice previously, to Ordoño III and Ordoño I
1 CONC V of León, from both of whom she separated. Sancho was her third and last husband. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT García IV of Pamplona 
1 CONT Ramiro (died 992) 
1 CONT Gonzalo, was given the county of Aragon under the regency of his mother 
1 CONT Urraca (Abda) the Basque, given to Al-Mansur before entering a convent 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT García III King of Pamplona
1 CONT 970–994 Succeeded by
1 CONT García IV 
1 CONT Count of Aragon
1 CONT 970–994 
0 @N6000000002322339919@ NOTE Defeated by the Moors 973 & 981, he submitted to the caliphate; visited
1 CONT Cordoba 992 to pay homage to Almanzor, who married a daughter of Sancho.
