0 @N6000000002188162541@ NOTE Bernard II of Toulouse
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernard II (died 877), called the Calf, was the Count of Toulouse, Rouergue, Limoges, Nîmes, Carcassonne, Razès, and Albi. He was the son of Raymond I and Bertha. The dates of his reign are disputed: either 865–877 or 864–872.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 863, his father, loyal to Charles the Bald, was deposed by Humfrid, Count of Barcelona. In 865, when his father died and Humfrid fled, Charles appointed him to the counties of Toulouse and Rouergue, probably including Pallars a
1 CONC nd Ribagorza, and Limoges. In 872, following the deposition of Oliba II of Carcassonne, he was given the counties of Albi, Nîmes, Carcassonne, and Razès. In 877, he was assassinated by a vassal of Bernard Plantapilosa, who took ov
1 CONC er Toulouse and Limoges. In Limoges, however, Bernard's brother Fulcoald, with the title of viscount, continued to exercise the power of government and is the founder of that great vicecomital family. Meanwhile, Oliba was reinstat
1 CONC ed in his old counties. In Pallars and Ribagorza, one Raymond I succeeded in separating those countships from the rest and inaugurating a new and de facto independent dynastic authority in the region.
0 @N6000000000437081472@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Count Name Suffix:<NSFX> of Toulouse
0 @N6000000002187918342@ NOTE Odo of Toulouse
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Odo or Eudes (also Odon or Odonus) was the Count of Toulouse from 872 to 918 or 919, when he died.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a son of Raymond I of Toulouse and Bertha[1] or of Bernard II of Toulouse.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Garsenda, daughter of Ermengol of Albi, and probably had three children. His sons were Raymond II, whom he associated in the countship by giving him Rouergue (before 898), and Ermengol, who inherited that same province.
1 CONC  It has been suggested for onomastic reasons that Odo was the father of Garsenda, wife of Wilfred II of Barcelona.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ FMG.
1 CONT    2. ^ Lewis, p 108.
1 CONT    3. ^ Suggested by Szabolcs de Vajay in "Comtesses d'origine occitane dans la Marche d'Espagne aux 10e and 11e siècles. Essai sur le rattachement de Richilde, de Garsende et de Letgardis, comtesses de Barcelone, et de Thietberge
1 CONC  comtesse d'Urgel au contexte généalogique occitan," Hidalguía 28 (1980), pp. 585-616, 601-2. [1]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718–1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965.
