0 @N6000000003637338279@ NOTE Fruela Petriz Bermúdez, es el que según Argáiz, dio al monasterio de San Martín de Jubia el de San Julián de Mondego, que era fundación de sus antecesores. Lo dio porque estando preso y con grandes trabajos en tierra de moros, se 
1 CONC encomendó a San Martín y lo sacó de ellos, como se dice en la donación. Casó con Doña Elvira de Faro, hija de Mendo Bermúdez de Faro y de su mujer Da. Ilduara Gutiérrez de Aranga, nieta paterna del conde Bermudo Fruela y la condes
1 CONC a Da. Gimena y nieta materna del conde D. Gutierre Rodríguez de Aranga y de la condesa Da. Gundesina. Según otras fuentes dice que fallecio en la conquista de Santarem.
1 CONT También aparece casado con Brioolania y con Urraca Fruela Condesa de Aranga
0 @N6000000002187750873@ NOTE Sancho II of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho II (1040-1072), called the Strong, or in Spanish, el Fuerte, was King of Castile (1065-1072) and León (1072). He was the eldest son of Ferdinand I of Castile and succeeded in Castile while his younger brother Alfonso succee
1 CONC ded in their mother's inheritance of León and Galicia was given to the youngest son García.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1068, Sancho defeated his cousins Sancho IV of Navarre and Sancho of Aragón in the War of the Three Sanchos. He reconquered Bureba, Alta Rioja, and Álava which his father had given to Sancho of Navarre's father, García, in retu
1 CONC rn for aid against Bermudo III of León. In that year, he defeated Alfonso, his brother, at Llantada, but he soon teamed up with him to conquer Galicia. They succeeded (1071) and partitioned it, but Sancho then turned on Alfonso. W
1 CONC ith the aid of his alférez El Cid, he defeated Alfonso at Golpejera (1072). He then forced him into exile in Toledo and took over Castile as king. His sister Urraca, Lady of Zamora, held that city against him, as the Leonese were 
1 CONC strongly opposed to his presence. It was consequently a Zamoran noble who finally put and end to Sancho's campaigning success. Vellido Dolfos entered Sancho's camp as a pretendant deserter, separating Sancho from his guard as if t
1 CONC o tell him the secrets of the Zamoran defence, and assassinated him.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded in his kingdoms by the brother he displaced, Alfonso.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, mention should be made of the part these events play in the Cantar de Mío Cid.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ferdinand I King of Castile Succeeded by
1 CONT Alfonso VI
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Alfonso VI King of Leon Succeeded by
1 CONT Alfonso VI
