1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Sancho RAMÍREZ (b. before 1045--d. July 6, 1094, Huesca, Aragon), king of Aragon from 1063 to 1094 and of Pamplona (or Navarre; as SanchoV Ramírez) from 1076 to 1094, the son of Ramiro I of Aragon.
1 CONT After the murder of Sancho IV of Navarre, Sancho Ramírez, with Navarrese consent, became king of Navarre, forestalling the ambition of Alfonso VI of Castile to annex that kingdom. Sancho's main importance was as king of Aragon. Af
1 CONC ter his father's death (1063) fighting the Moors at Graus, the papacy organized three international crusades against Spanish Islam (1063, 1073, and 1087). They all failed, but Sancho reconquered many places from his own resources,
1 CONC  notably in the regions of Huesca and Monzón; at the end of his reign Aragon began to edge toward the Mediterranean coast. Sancho placed his kingdoms under the feudal protection of the Holy See in 1089. He died of wounds during th
1 CONC e siege of Huesca
0 @N6000000002187750395@ NOTE Sancho I of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho of Aragon (Spanish: Sancho Ramírez) (c. 1042 – June 4, 1094, Huesca) was king of Aragon from 1063 until 1094 as Sancho I and king of Navarre from 1076 until 1094) as Sancho V.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Ramiro I of Aragon and Ermesinde of Bigorre, and he succeeded his father in 1063. He was elected king of Navarre in 1076 after Sancho IV of Navarre was murdered by his own siblings, thus prompting a succession cr
1 CONC isis. Sancho's young son, Garcia, was recognized as titular king in Leon and Castile, where he lived in exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho conquered Barbastro in 1065, Graus in 1083, and Monzon in 1089.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married first in c.1065 (divorced 1071), Isabel of Urgel (d. c.1071), daughter of Count Armengol III of Urgel; and second in 1076, Felicie of Roucy (d May 3, 1123), daughter of Count Hilduin III of Roucy.
1 CONT
1 CONT His three sons, Pedro, Alfonso and Ramiro, succeeded in turn to the throne of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramiro I King of Aragon
1 CONT 1063–1094 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Peter I 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sancho IV King of Navarre
1 CONT 1076–1094 
0 @N6000000002322342246@ NOTE Became King of Navarre after murder of cousin Sancho IV in 1074. He died of
1 CONT wounds suffered during the seige of Huesca in 1094
0 @N6000000002176683778@ NOTE Became King of Navarre after murder of cousin Sancho IV in 1074. He died of
1 CONT wounds suffered during the seige of Huesca in 1094
0 @N6000000002240835246@ NOTE Became King of Navarre after murder of cousin Sancho IV in 1074. He died of
1 CONT wounds suffered during the seige of Huesca in 1094
0 @N6000000002244430061@ NOTE Became King of Navarre after murder of cousin Sancho IV in 1074. He died of
1 CONT wounds suffered during the seige of Huesca in 1094
0 @N6000000003051298693@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Sancho Ramírez, King of Aragón and Navarre", retrieved 16 Oct 07]
1 CONT Sancho Ramírez (c. 1042 ? 4 June 1094, Huesca) was king of Aragon (1063-1094, as Sancho I) and king of Navarre (1076-1094, as Sancho V). He was the son of Ramiro I of Aragon and Ermesinde of Bigorre, and he succeeded his father in
1 CONC  1063.
1 CONT
1 CONT Between 1067 and 1068, the War of the Three Sanchos involved him in a conflict with his first cousins, both also named Sancho: Sancho IV the king of Navarre and Sancho II the king of Castile, respectively. The Castilian Sancho was
1 CONC  trying to retake Bureba and Alta Rioja, which his father had given away to king of Navarre and failed to retake. The Navarrese Sancho begged the aid of the Aragonese Sancho to defend his kingdom. Sancho of Castile defeated the tw
1 CONC o cousins and retook both Bureba and Alta Rioja, as well as Álava.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho Ramírez was elected king of Navarre in 1076 after Sancho IV of Navarre was murdered by his own siblings, thus prompting a succession crisis. Sancho's young son, García, was recognized as titular king in León and Castile, wh
1 CONC ere he lived in exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho conquered Barbastro in 1064, Graus in 1083, and Monzón in 1089.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married first in c.1065 (divorced 1071), Isabel of Urgel (d. c.1071), daughter of Count Armengol III of Urgel and second in 1076, Felicie of Roucy (d May 3, 1123), daughter of Count Hilduin III of Roucy. A third marriage - to P
1 CONC hilippa of Toulouse - is sometimes given [1] but other evidence records him as still married to Felicie at the time of his death.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT He perished in 1094 at the Siege of Huesca.
1 CONT
1 CONT His three sons: by Isabel, he had Peter; by Felicie he had Alfonso and Ramiro. All three succeeded in turn to the throne of Aragon.
0 @N6000000003949868361@ NOTE Between 1067 and 1068, the War of the Three Sanchos involved him in a conflict with his first cousins, both also named Sancho: Sancho IV the king of Navarre and Sancho II the king of Castile, respectively. The Castilian Sancho was
1 CONC  trying to retake Bureba and Alta Rioja, which his father had given away to king of Navarre and failed to retake. The Navarrese Sancho begged the aid of the Aragonese Sancho to defend his kingdom. Sancho of Castile defeated the tw
1 CONC o cousins and retook both Bureba and Alta Rioja, as well as Álava.
1 CONT Sancho Ramírez was elected king of Navarre in 1076 after Sancho IV of Navarre was murdered by his own siblings, thus prompting a succession crisis. Sancho's young son, García, was recognized as titular king in León and Castile, wh
1 CONC ere he lived in exile.
1 CONT Sancho conquered Barbastro in 1065, Graus in 1083, and Monzón in 1089.
0 @N6000000005598760931@ NOTE Sancho was proclaimed King of Aragon in 1063 and two years later attac ked the Moslems.  In this attack, he was aided by French troops and af ter a difficult siege made himself master of Balbastro which united fo r a time the area
1 CONC s of Navarre with Aragon.  It is reported that Sanch o married second, after 1086,  Phillippe, the daughter of William IV o f Toulouse. He died while laying siege at Huesca.
