0 @N6000000002187918305@ NOTE Ermengol of Rouergue
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermengol or Ermengaud (died after July 935) was a son of Odo of Toulouse and Garsindis. His father gave him the County of Rouergue and Quercy in 906 and he governed it to his death. His brother was Raymond II of Toulouse and toget
1 CONC her they governed the vast patrimony of their house in the first half of the tenth century.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 930, he donated property to the abbey of Vabres in a charter dated to the seventh year of King Rudolph bearing only the title of comes (count). In January 932, he made a similar donation with the title of princeps (prince). He 
1 CONC was probably regarded as princeps Gothiae, a title which was to run in his family in the tenth century. He and his nephew Raymond Pons of Toulouse, together with Sancho IV of Gascony, went to the court of Rudolph that year to do h
1 CONC omage for their lands.[1] This did not have the desired effect, however, of satisfying royal desires for influence in the south and Rudolph accompanied Ebalus of Aquitaine against the Vikings a short while later, strengthening the
1 CONC  Ramnulfid dynasty against that of the Rouergue in the fight for supremacy in Aquitaine.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ermengol and his wife Adelais (Adalaiz) had two known sons and one daughter, though charters of his eldest son indicate that he had other sons besides his two heirs. The eldest son was Raymond, who inherited Rouergue, and the seco
1 CONC nd was Hugh, who received Quercy. His daughter is hypthesised to have married Sunifred II, Count of Barcelona.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Flodoard gives this duke as Lupus Aznar Vasco, but Lewis, 184n, takes this as a refernce to a duke of Gascony named Sánchez.
1 CONT    2. ^ Lewis, 187.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718–1050. University of Texas Press: Austin, 1965.
